imagine as you're walking down the  streets thoughts pop into your head  picturing a stranger naked imagining  having sex with your boss jumping in  front of a train they're upsetting  intrusive thoughts but imagine they  don't just go away they get stronger  more disturbing graphic sexual and  violent thoughts they make you question  who you are stuck on a loop inside your  head  this is OCD but not as you know it  loads of people think they know what OCD  is all about as that joke that  somebody's quite organized or tidy  they're a little bit OCD but I started  coming across posts and blogs and there  seem to be a secret side to this a  hidden form of OCD and often people  would start suffering with this when  they were very young and they would live  with it for decades so we've decided to  go and meet some of the people who  suffer with this condition  it pretty much happened overnight I was  15 years old and I was having dinner  with my parents my brother  and that's when I experienced my first  ever intrusive obsessive thought the  mental image that I'd had was a naked  child of a naked little boy and you  don't when you have those kind of  thoughts you don't speak about them I  thought I'd committed some horrendous  act of thought crime just by having had  that thought so it was immediately  something which felt unacceptable and  alien to me and very frightening so  right from the very start it was  something that I was absolutely not  prepared to talk about everything that I  did for 11 and a half years was  underscored by this constant voice  constant doubt constant rapid-fire  mental images now at the time you didn't  he didn't know it was OCD so you must  have you must be terrified I sort of  hate on something recently it the only  way I can kind of describe that terror  is it felt like I I killed someone and I  had a body in the back guard and that's  what it felt like that was the kind of  terror that oh my god I've done  something horrendous I've sort of  thought something so terrible I've  committed an unforgivable sin and my  life is never going to be the same again  that was the terror it was it was it was  it was doom really it was nightmarish  petros socially you'd have no idea any  of that was going on for her under the  service that's probably what makes this  hard to recognize as OCD it's not  obvious like perhaps washing or checking  or cleaning so what makes this OCD and  what's going on under the surface the  content of these sorts of thoughts are  commonly about sex  in terms of being a pedophile also about  violence you know that they might  assault someone or sometimes blasphemy  and in moral question it's just a part  of being human it's normal we all get  ridiculous intrusive thoughts and images  and doubts that just pop into our head  no one with OCD has ever been recorded  to act upon their intrusive thoughts and  images or doubts it just hasn't happened  and there's adequate good treatment for  OCD which means that these thoughts and  worries will go away obsessive  compulsive disorder is a vicious cycle  first unwanted intrusive thoughts occur  repeatedly these are the obsessions they  make the person increasingly anxious  often fixating upon the fear they are  bad or could do something terrible they  feel they must use mental and physical  rituals to cancel the thoughts out these  are the compulsions but attempts to get  rid of the thoughts feeds the original  obsession allowing OCD to tighten its  grip the intrusive thoughts represent a  person's worst fears as opposed to any  desire to carry them out  because of the secretive nature of this  form of OCD nicknamed pure oh no  official data exists but to get an idea  of how many people are affected the  charity OCD action counted the number of  calls and emails they received over 48  days over a third were from people with  violent sexual or religious intrusive  thoughts or who said they had pure Oh  many are scared to seek help in case  they might be misunderstood by mental  health staff or doctors  and that's what happened to Adam a  teacher who's asked to remain anonymous  it's incredibly hard to talk about these  things they're like my absolute worst  fears the example I gave I said there  might be a worry that I'm a pedophile  because that's basically the worst thing  that you can be I know that I'm not it's  ridiculous but it's the worst thing that  I could be so that's why I'm worried  about it he claims his therapist knew he  had a history of OCD and that his  thoughts were distressing not  pleasurable despite this he was reported  to safeguarding services and unable to  go to work the whole time it was framed  as a risk assessment that until I'd had  a risk assessment they couldn't put me  back because they'd obviously been this  red flag waves against me they said look  you're not going to be suspended but we  don't feel it's best you come to school  my initial thought was am I going to  prison it's easily the most traumatic  thing that's ever happened in my life  after being referred externally to a  safeguarding charity Adam was cleared of  all concerns he since returned to work  as a teacher and he says the health  ombudsman is now investigating his  treatment  what does it do to them if somebody's  put through that process and they've got  OCD what does that actually do to them  well at best they may just be very angry  and frustrated by it all at worst it's  going to make their OCD far worse lose  trust in the professionals and may  completely ruin their life in terms of  lost jobs or being taken away from their  children all sorts of nonsense so it  really is disastrous just hearing about  the nature of the thoughts that OCD can  plague you with you can get a sense of  how scary and how how terrifying that  might be but couple that with the  additional pressure of not only worrying  about yourself but having a young baby  and a new family and you can now you can  imagine how OCD takes on a whole  different level so we've come to  Birmingham now to meet with a young mom  who knows and is all about the pressure  of having a young baby and OCD like a  constant 24/7 movie in my head of  everything violent and graphic you could  ever do to yourself or someone else and  if one starts another one starts and it  can be anything you know anything that  you never want to think about something  me her in this tiny newborn baby that  and I'm you're always going to do it but  it was like a horror movie playing in my  head of all the horrible things I was  going to do so I stopped going out  because I was convinced you know I'd put  him out in the middle of a road or  someone would take him I stopped letting  people in in case they did something  when did you finally find out that you  weren't a bad person what Mads you had  OCD two years later add my initial  assessment and they said you know you  tick every box for OCD everything you  have said to us today is OCD and I was  with my first it was like I'm really  messy I don't know just happened like  when you look back now it's so obvious  what was wrong  and I think there was a few missed  opportunities to you know look a bit  deeper than just what I'd come in with  that day so national guidelines do exist  and are in place for how to recognize  and treat all forms of OCD but still  many of the people who spoke to me and  who work in this field feel there just  isn't the understanding of the true  range of forms OCD can take and they are  calling increasingly for things to  change and it's honestly devastating to  hear of anyone that is finding it so  tough but it's also wonderful they brave  enough to speak to you to raise that  awareness I'm worried about those people  that can't come forward or haven't had  the opportunity to have their voice  heard and we can point to some really  excellent services in some parts of the  country but that's not commonplace and  there's also too many examples that I've  hear